# TWO files, and the split is not cosmetic.
#
#   .env       what COMPOSE substitutes into docker-compose.yml. Compose
#              interpolates ${...} inside these values, so nothing containing a
#              literal $ may live here.
#   auth.env   what the APP is handed, via env_file, byte for byte. The scrypt
#              hash is full of $ — `$iwiQfO…` reads as a variable reference and
#              would expand to nothing — so it belongs here and nowhere else.
#
# It also keeps TUNNEL_TOKEN out of the app's container, which has no business
# being able to stand up the tunnel.
#
# Copy both, fill them in, and chmod 600 both:
#
#   cp .env.example .env && cp auth.env.example auth.env && chmod 600 .env auth.env

# ── the public endpoint (this file: .env) ───────────────────────────────────

# The single public hostname this server may be addressed by. Leave empty for a
# LAN-only install. With it set, the anti-DNS-rebinding guard lets that ONE name
# through — which is safe only because a session, not the Host header, is what
# grants access from then on.
#
# The server REFUSES TO START with this set and no password hash in auth.env.
KINOPUB_PUBLIC_HOST=

# Cloudflare Tunnel token, from the dashboard (Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels).
# Kept here rather than on the cloudflared command line, where `ps` would show
# it to every user on the box. Base64, no $, so interpolation cannot touch it.
#
# Configure the tunnel's public hostname to point at http://kinopub-gui:8765 —
# the service name on this compose project's own network, so nothing has to be
# port-forwarded.
TUNNEL_TOKEN=
